items.body:
`requireContentType()` documents a 422 response for malformed Content-Type values, but only checks whether the type has a nonempty substring on either side of its first slash. Wildcard acceptance therefore lets malformed media types through.

Reproduced on current main, Node 24:

```ts
import { H3, requireContentType } from 'h3'

const app = new H3()
app.post('/', event => ({
  mediaType: requireContentType(event, ['application/*']),
}))

const response = await app.fetch(new Request('http://localhost/', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json/extra' },
}))
console.log(response.status) // 200; expected 422
```

`application/json junk` and `application/(json)` are also accepted by that wildcard. With an exact accepted type, malformed values reach the unsupported-type branch instead of the documented malformed-type branch.

The type and subtype must each be an HTTP token ([RFC 9110, sections 8.3.1 and 5.6.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-media-type)). I plan to add complete type/subtype token validation before matching accepted types, with regression tests in both web and Node modes plus valid punctuation/parameter/wildcard controls. No parameter-parsing or API changes are intended. This work is AI-assisted.
